1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:13,984
Right now, the air around you is carrying thousands of conversations — police dispatch, aircraft, weather
satellites, your neighbor's doorbell — all at once, all invisible, all riding ripples in the electromagnetic field.

2
00:00:13,904 --> 00:00:29,065
In the next half hour you'll learn the physics that makes that possible: what a radio wave actually is, the three knobs every
transmitter turns, why spectrum is priced like real estate, and the one number that decides whether a signal decodes or dies.

3
00:00:28,985 --> 00:00:32,757
This is Radio Fundamentals — how signals
actually work.

4
00:00:34,130 --> 00:00:34,987
Welcome.

5
00:00:34,907 --> 00:00:48,861
This course is for anyone pointing a software-defined radio at the sky — scanner listeners, new hams, tinkerers with a
thirty-dollar dongle — and for anyone who's ever nodded along to "megahertz" without being totally sure what it measures.

6
00:00:48,781 --> 00:00:52,006
No math beyond arithmetic, no
prerequisites.

7
00:00:51,926 --> 00:00:58,089
Just the mental models underneath every antenna,
every waterfall display, and every decode.

8
00:00:58,239 --> 00:01:00,722
Six chapters, each one idea.

9
00:01:00,642 --> 00:01:04,135
First, the radio wave itself — what's
actually rippling.

10
00:01:04,055 --> 00:01:07,559
Then frequency, the address every signal
lives at.

11
00:01:07,479 --> 00:01:11,355
Then modulation — how a wave is made to
carry a message.

12
00:01:11,275 --> 00:01:14,617
Then bandwidth, the width of the road it
drives on.

13
00:01:14,537 --> 00:01:22,883
And finally the measuring tools: the decibel, radio's own arithmetic,
and signal-to-noise ratio, the number that decides everything.

14
00:01:23,033 --> 00:01:34,037
Every chapter stands alone, and each one matches a written Field Guide article at gophertrunk
dot org — linked below, chapter by chapter, if you want the formulas and the sources.

15
00:01:33,957 --> 00:01:35,348
Skip freely.

16
00:01:35,268 --> 00:01:38,726
But watched in order, each idea hands off
to the next.

17
00:01:38,646 --> 00:01:40,548
Let's start with the ripple itself.

18
00:01:43,730 --> 00:01:50,311
A radio wave is a ripple — not in water, but in
the electromagnetic field that fills all of space.

19
00:01:50,231 --> 00:01:53,561
Drop a stone in a pond and rings spread
outward.

20
00:01:53,481 --> 00:02:03,336
Feed an alternating current into an antenna, and the same thing happens to the
invisible field around it: a disturbance detaches and races away at the speed of light.

21
00:02:03,256 --> 00:02:10,534
Every wireless thing you own — your phone, your car fob,
a police radio — is throwing stones into that same pond.

22
00:02:10,684 --> 00:02:20,400
Formally, a radio wave is electromagnetic radiation in the radio range of the
spectrum — by convention, about three kilohertz up to three hundred gigahertz.

23
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:23,081
It is the same physical phenomenon as
light.

24
00:02:23,001 --> 00:02:29,117
Radio, microwaves, infrared, visible
light: one family, different frequencies.

25
00:02:29,037 --> 00:02:36,187
Radio just sits at the low, long-wavelength end — the
part that bends around hills and passes through walls.

26
00:02:36,337 --> 00:02:37,636
Here is the mechanism.

27
00:02:37,556 --> 00:02:41,815
The accelerating charges in the antenna
create an oscillating electric field.

28
00:02:41,735 --> 00:02:46,435
A changing electric field generates a
magnetic field at right angles to it.

29
00:02:46,355 --> 00:02:49,720
And a changing magnetic field regenerates
the electric field.

30
00:02:49,640 --> 00:02:57,486
The two leapfrog each other, detach from the antenna, and
self-propagate — no wires, no air, no medium needed at all.

31
00:02:57,406 --> 00:03:00,795
That is why radio crosses the vacuum of
space just fine.

32
00:03:00,945 --> 00:03:03,811
Freeze one of those waves and it looks
like this.

33
00:03:03,731 --> 00:03:05,993
Three properties describe it completely.

34
00:03:05,913 --> 00:03:08,593
Amplitude — how strong the wave is.

35
00:03:08,513 --> 00:03:11,773
Frequency — how many times it cycles each
second.

36
00:03:11,693 --> 00:03:15,128
And phase — where in its cycle it is at a
given instant.

37
00:03:15,048 --> 00:03:19,899
Hold on to those three, because they are the
only three things a transmitter can manipulate.

38
00:03:19,819 --> 00:03:32,159
Every radio scheme ever invented — AM, FM, the digital modulation in a trunked police system
— is just a different way of wiggling amplitude, frequency, or phase in step with a message.

39
00:03:32,309 --> 00:03:37,914
Out in the world, the ripple spreads and weakens
— power thins out with the square of distance.

40
00:03:37,834 --> 00:03:45,495
By the time it reaches a receiving antenna ten miles away,
the passing field induces a current of just a few microvolts.

41
00:03:45,415 --> 00:03:49,895
That whisper is what a receiver amplifies,
filters, and decodes.

42
00:03:49,815 --> 00:03:58,567
Everything in radio engineering — antennas, amplifiers, the decibel math
coming later in this course — exists to protect that whisper on its trip.

43
00:03:58,717 --> 00:04:01,501
And this is where GopherTrunk picks up the
story.

44
00:04:01,421 --> 00:04:16,478
A software-defined radio doesn't decode the wave directly: its front end mixes a slice of spectrum down and digitizes
it into a stream of IQ samples — complex numbers that capture the wave's amplitude and phase at every instant.

45
00:04:16,398 --> 00:04:20,215
From that point on, the physical wave has
become arithmetic.

46
00:04:20,135 --> 00:04:25,799
But every property you just met survives the conversion
— and recovering the message means tracking all three.

47
00:04:26,749 --> 00:04:39,402
So: a radio wave is a self-propagating ripple in the electromagnetic field, it lives between three
kilohertz and three hundred gigahertz, and it gives a transmitter exactly three knobs to turn.

48
00:04:39,322 --> 00:04:41,677
The full write-up is linked below.

49
00:04:45,330 --> 00:04:48,196
So a wave is a ripple with three
properties.

50
00:04:48,116 --> 00:04:53,896
The first one — how fast it wiggles — is so
important it gets a whole chapter: frequency.

51
00:04:56,460 --> 00:05:00,034
One hundred sixty-two point five five zero
megahertz.

52
00:04:59,954 --> 00:05:05,746
Tune there, almost anywhere in the United States, and
you'll hear the robotic voice of a weather station.

53
00:05:05,666 --> 00:05:13,617
That number — the one thing everybody knows about a radio signal
— is its frequency: how many times the wave cycles each second.

54
00:05:13,537 --> 00:05:15,915
Get it right and the signal is yours.

55
00:05:15,835 --> 00:05:18,457
Get it wrong by a hair and you hear
nothing at all.

56
00:05:18,607 --> 00:05:28,392
Frequency is measured in hertz — one hertz is one cycle per second, named for Heinrich
Hertz, who first generated and detected radio waves in the eighteen-eighties.

57
00:05:28,312 --> 00:05:38,666
It's the inverse of the cycle's duration: a signal at one hundred megahertz
repeats a hundred million times a second, so each cycle lasts just ten nanoseconds.

58
00:05:38,816 --> 00:05:41,926
Picture two waves side by side for one
second.

59
00:05:41,846 --> 00:05:50,226
The low-frequency wave fits a handful of cycles into that
second; the high-frequency wave packs in thousands of times more.

60
00:05:50,146 --> 00:05:54,696
Same speed of travel — always the speed of
light — just a faster wiggle.

61
00:05:54,616 --> 00:06:04,436
And since radio numbers get big fast, we scale the unit: kilohertz for
thousands, megahertz for millions, gigahertz for billions of cycles per second.

62
00:06:04,586 --> 00:06:10,621
Frequency has a twin: wavelength — the
physical distance one cycle covers in flight.

63
00:06:10,541 --> 00:06:20,187
Because every radio wave travels at the same speed, the two are locked
on a seesaw: wavelength equals the speed of light divided by frequency.

64
00:06:20,107 --> 00:06:23,101
Double the frequency and the wavelength
halves.

65
00:06:23,021 --> 00:06:34,455
An FM broadcast station at one hundred megahertz has waves three meters long; a UHF
public-safety channel near four hundred sixty megahertz, about sixty-five centimeters.

66
00:06:34,375 --> 00:06:40,932
That's why antennas come in different sizes — an antenna wants to
match its wave, and we'll spend a whole chapter on that later.

67
00:06:41,082 --> 00:06:47,710
Lay every possible frequency along one axis and you get
the radio spectrum — and its neighborhoods have names.

68
00:06:47,630 --> 00:06:52,504
HF, the shortwave bands that bounce off
the upper atmosphere and cross oceans.

69
00:06:52,424 --> 00:06:56,834
VHF, home of FM broadcast, aircraft, and
marine radio.

70
00:06:56,754 --> 00:07:02,464
UHF, where most trunked public-safety
systems, cell phones, and Wi-Fi live.

71
00:07:02,384 --> 00:07:07,990
A frequency isn't just a number — it's an
address, with physics and regulations attached.

72
00:07:08,140 --> 00:07:15,011
For an SDR, frequency is the first thing you set: it
decides what slice of spectrum lands in the capture.

73
00:07:14,931 --> 00:07:21,919
But here's the catch — cheap tuner hardware runs slightly
off its dial frequency, by a few parts per million.

74
00:07:21,839 --> 00:07:33,052
GopherTrunk, like any serious decoder, applies a correction and then continuously tracks the
residual error while it decodes, because at these precisions, oscillators drift with temperature.

75
00:07:32,972 --> 00:07:37,951
Getting the frequency right, and keeping it
right, is the first requirement for any decode.

76
00:07:38,901 --> 00:07:48,419
So: frequency is cycles per second, it's chained to wavelength through the speed
of light, and it places every signal into a named neighborhood of the spectrum.

77
00:07:48,339 --> 00:07:50,207
Full write-up linked below.

78
00:07:53,860 --> 00:07:57,097
You can now find any signal's address on
the spectrum.

79
00:07:57,017 --> 00:08:01,416
But a wave sitting at an address,
perfectly steady, says nothing at all.

80
00:08:01,336 --> 00:08:04,120
Making it talk is next: modulation.

81
00:08:06,690 --> 00:08:13,201
Modulation is the answer to an embarrassing question:
a perfect radio wave says absolutely nothing.

82
00:08:13,121 --> 00:08:21,525
A pure, steady carrier is just a hum in the spectrum — endlessly
repeating, perfectly predictable, carrying zero information.

83
00:08:21,445 --> 00:08:24,427
To make a wave say something, you have to
change it.

84
00:08:24,347 --> 00:08:32,402
Modulation is the art of changing a carrier wave in step with a message
— and every radio scheme in history is a variation on that one move.

85
00:08:32,552 --> 00:08:42,965
Remember the three properties from the last chapter's write-up — a sine wave has an
amplitude, a frequency, and a phase, and those are the only things a transmitter can touch.

86
00:08:42,885 --> 00:08:45,762
So there are only three families of
modulation.

87
00:08:45,682 --> 00:08:51,044
Everything from nineteen-twenties broadcasting
to your phone is built from these three knobs.

88
00:08:51,194 --> 00:08:59,401
Turn the amplitude knob and you get AM: the message is written into
the carrier's height — its envelope literally traces the audio.

89
00:08:59,321 --> 00:09:05,960
Simple, but noise also lives in amplitude,
which is why AM crackles in a thunderstorm.

90
00:09:05,880 --> 00:09:16,663
Turn the frequency knob and you get FM: the message speeds up and slows down the carrier's
wiggle while the height stays constant — so amplitude noise mostly wipes right off.

91
00:09:16,583 --> 00:09:28,772
That constant envelope is why FM sounds cleaner than AM, and the third knob, phase, nudges
the carrier's timing — closely related to FM, and the foundation of almost everything digital.

92
00:09:28,922 --> 00:09:32,357
Analog modulation varies the carrier
continuously.

93
00:09:32,277 --> 00:09:42,712
Digital modulation makes one crucial change: it switches the carrier among
a finite menu of states, called symbols, each standing for one or more bits.

94
00:09:42,632 --> 00:09:47,739
Shift between discrete frequencies and
it's FSK — frequency-shift keying.

95
00:09:47,659 --> 00:09:49,909
Discrete phases: PSK.

96
00:09:49,829 --> 00:09:53,229
Combine amplitude and phase levels: QAM.

97
00:09:53,149 --> 00:10:05,047
Draw the menu as dots on a plane — amplitude out from the center, phase around it — and you get
the constellation diagram, the map every digital receiver uses to decide which symbol just arrived.

98
00:10:05,197 --> 00:10:08,377
And here is the trade that shapes all of
digital radio.

99
00:10:08,297 --> 00:10:13,635
More dots on the map means more bits per
symbol — faster data in the same bandwidth.

100
00:10:13,555 --> 00:10:21,147
But more dots also means the dots sit closer together,
so it takes less noise to smudge one into its neighbor.

101
00:10:21,067 --> 00:10:24,246
Fast-but-fragile, or slow-but-tough.

102
00:10:24,166 --> 00:10:31,699
Wi-Fi at close range runs hundreds of points; a police
radio fighting fades across a whole city runs just four.

103
00:10:31,849 --> 00:10:35,411
That's exactly what GopherTrunk spends its
life demodulating.

104
00:10:35,331 --> 00:10:53,812
The land-mobile systems it decodes chose rugged, low-order schemes on purpose: four-level FSK — four frequency steps, two bits
per symbol — for DMR and NXDN, and a rotating four-phase scheme called pi-over-four DQPSK for TETRA and P25's simulcast flavor.

105
00:10:53,732 --> 00:11:02,833
Once the signal is digitized, demodulation is just measuring amplitude,
frequency, and phase — the same three knobs — and mapping them back to bits.

106
00:11:03,783 --> 00:11:15,089
So: modulation turns a mute carrier into a messenger, there are exactly three knobs to
turn, and digital radio is a menu of symbol states traded off between speed and toughness.

107
00:11:15,009 --> 00:11:16,981
Full write-up linked below.

108
00:11:20,660 --> 00:11:27,787
Here's the catch modulation doesn't advertise: the moment
a carrier starts talking, it spreads out and takes up room.

109
00:11:27,707 --> 00:11:31,617
How much room — and why that room is so
expensive — is bandwidth.

110
00:11:34,190 --> 00:11:39,447
Radio spectrum is real estate — and bandwidth
is how much of it a signal occupies.

111
00:11:39,367 --> 00:11:44,195
A narrowband voice channel takes about
twelve and a half kilohertz of road.

112
00:11:44,115 --> 00:11:49,071
An FM broadcast station, two hundred
kilohertz — sixteen times wider.

113
00:11:48,991 --> 00:11:51,880
A Wi-Fi channel, tens of megahertz.

114
00:11:51,800 --> 00:11:59,890
Bandwidth decides how fast information can flow, how many signals
fit side by side, and how much spectrum a receiver has to swallow.

115
00:11:59,810 --> 00:12:02,734
It may be the most consequential number in
radio.

116
00:12:02,884 --> 00:12:08,978
Formally: bandwidth is the width, in hertz,
of the frequency range a signal occupies.

117
00:12:09,128 --> 00:12:11,355
Why does a signal have width at all?

118
00:12:11,275 --> 00:12:15,708
An unmodulated carrier is a single
spectral line — zero width.

119
00:12:15,628 --> 00:12:23,114
The moment you modulate it, energy spreads into sidebands on either
side, and the faster the message changes, the wider they spread.

120
00:12:23,034 --> 00:12:24,739
Information takes room.

121
00:12:24,659 --> 00:12:27,688
That's not an engineering flaw; it's
physics.

122
00:12:27,608 --> 00:12:38,310
Regulators then draw lane markings: each service gets a channel of a defined width, with
guard bands of empty spectrum between lanes to keep neighbors from sideswiping each other.

123
00:12:38,460 --> 00:12:41,268
And here's the law that makes bandwidth
precious.

124
00:12:41,188 --> 00:12:50,695
Shannon's theorem sets the ceiling on any channel's data rate: capacity
equals bandwidth times the log of one plus the signal-to-noise ratio.

125
00:12:50,615 --> 00:12:52,401
Look at the shape of that formula.

126
00:12:52,321 --> 00:12:57,241
Capacity grows linearly with bandwidth —
double the width, double the ceiling.

127
00:12:57,161 --> 00:13:02,384
But only logarithmically with signal power —
doubling your SNR buys you a mere fraction more.

128
00:13:02,304 --> 00:13:09,106
If you want more data, more bandwidth is the straight road,
which is exactly why spectrum auctions raise billions.

129
00:13:09,256 --> 00:13:11,425
Bandwidth cuts the other way too.

130
00:13:11,345 --> 00:13:18,750
Noise power grows with every hertz you listen to — open your receiver's
filter wider than the signal and all you admit is more hiss.

131
00:13:18,670 --> 00:13:27,248
The craft on the receive side is matching the filter to the signal: wide
enough to pass it undistorted, narrow enough to shut out everything else.

132
00:13:27,168 --> 00:13:31,334
Keep that idea — it becomes the star of
the signal-to-noise chapter.

133
00:13:31,484 --> 00:13:34,873
GopherTrunk lives on both sides of this
trade at once.

134
00:13:34,793 --> 00:13:44,055
Its SDR captures a slice several megahertz wide — wide enough to watch a
trunked system's control channel and all its voice channels simultaneously.

135
00:13:43,975 --> 00:13:52,391
Then digital down-converters carve out each narrow twelve-and-a-half-kilohertz
channel and filter it down to just the bandwidth the decoder needs.

136
00:13:52,311 --> 00:13:56,280
Wide capture to see everything; narrow
channels to decode cleanly.

137
00:13:56,200 --> 00:13:58,264
That split is trunk-tracking.

138
00:13:59,214 --> 00:14:09,127
So: bandwidth is a signal's footprint on the spectrum, the linear lever in Shannon's
speed limit, and — on receive — a filter you keep just as narrow as you can.

139
00:14:09,047 --> 00:14:11,112
Full write-up linked below.

140
00:14:14,790 --> 00:14:17,667
We just compared powers that differ by
billions.

141
00:14:17,587 --> 00:14:22,740
Radio has its own arithmetic for exactly that
— small numbers you can add in your head.

142
00:14:22,660 --> 00:14:23,866
Meet the decibel.

143
00:14:26,420 --> 00:14:29,959
Decibels exist because radio numbers are
ridiculous.

144
00:14:29,879 --> 00:14:38,097
The signal your antenna picks up from a repeater ten miles away can be a hundred
billion times weaker than the signal leaving that repeater's transmitter.

145
00:14:38,017 --> 00:14:41,080
Nobody wants to do math with eleven zeros.

146
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:46,142
The decibel is how radio engineers make
those numbers small, friendly — and addable.

147
00:14:46,292 --> 00:14:51,224
A decibel is a logarithmic way of writing
a ratio between two power levels.

148
00:14:51,144 --> 00:14:53,255
Not an amount — a ratio.

149
00:14:53,175 --> 00:14:56,053
Ten decibels means ten times the power.

150
00:14:55,973 --> 00:14:58,653
Twenty decibels means a hundred times.

151
00:14:58,573 --> 00:15:00,243
Thirty means a thousand.

152
00:15:00,163 --> 00:15:03,946
Every ten decibels you add multiplies the
power by another ten.

153
00:15:04,096 --> 00:15:06,033
The everyday anchor is a piano.

154
00:15:05,953 --> 00:15:11,210
Each octave is the same-sized step to your
ear, but each one doubles the frequency.

155
00:15:11,130 --> 00:15:16,028
Decibels do that for power: equal steps
that stand for equal multiplications.

156
00:15:15,948 --> 00:15:22,355
Your ear and your radio both live on that kind of scale,
which is why decibels feel natural once they click.

157
00:15:22,505 --> 00:15:24,860
Three numbers are worth memorizing.

158
00:15:24,780 --> 00:15:27,343
Plus three decibels: double the power.

159
00:15:27,263 --> 00:15:29,119
Minus three: half.

160
00:15:29,039 --> 00:15:31,139
Plus ten: ten times.

161
00:15:31,059 --> 00:15:34,099
That's it — every other value is a
combination.

162
00:15:34,019 --> 00:15:35,619
Sixteen decibels?

163
00:15:35,539 --> 00:15:41,307
That's ten plus three plus three: ten times
two times two — forty times the power.

164
00:15:41,227 --> 00:15:44,232
You just did logarithms in your head
without noticing.

165
00:15:44,382 --> 00:15:49,326
And here's the superpower: on a log scale,
multiplication becomes addition.

166
00:15:49,246 --> 00:15:59,670
Follow a signal from transmitter to receiver — every cable loses a few decibels,
every antenna adds a few, the path between them takes away a hundred or more.

167
00:15:59,590 --> 00:16:05,765
Instead of multiplying eleven-digit numbers, you
just add and subtract small ones down the chain.

168
00:16:05,685 --> 00:16:11,198
That running sum is called a link budget, and
it's how every radio link on Earth is designed.

169
00:16:11,348 --> 00:16:12,403
One trap.

170
00:16:12,323 --> 00:16:17,232
A plain decibel is always relative — a
ratio between two things.

171
00:16:17,152 --> 00:16:31,732
The moment you see a letter after it, it's become an absolute number, measured against a fixed reference:
d-B-m means compared to one milliwatt; d-B-F-S means compared to the loudest sample your SDR can represent.

172
00:16:31,652 --> 00:16:33,659
Same math, different anchor.

173
00:16:33,809 --> 00:16:45,695
You'll see all three flavors in GopherTrunk's dashboard: signal level in d-B-F-S,
signal-to-noise ratio in plain decibels, and hardware specs quoted in d-B-m.

174
00:16:45,615 --> 00:16:49,596
Now they're not three mysteries — they're
one idea with three anchors.

175
00:16:50,546 --> 00:17:00,192
So: decibels turn absurd ratios into small numbers, multiplication
into addition, and three memorized steps into fluent mental math.

176
00:17:00,112 --> 00:17:03,593
The full write-up, with the formulas, is
linked below.

177
00:17:07,250 --> 00:17:13,831
Armed with decibels, you can finally ask radio's
bottom-line question: how far above the noise is my signal?

178
00:17:13,751 --> 00:17:16,803
One last chapter: signal-to-noise ratio.

179
00:17:19,380 --> 00:17:23,093
Signal-to-noise ratio is the number that
decides everything.

180
00:17:23,013 --> 00:17:36,421
Every receiver on Earth is listening to a party it can't leave: below every signal hisses a floor of
noise — thermal noise from physics itself, plus every switching power supply and LED billboard in town.

181
00:17:36,341 --> 00:17:42,051
Whether your signal decodes comes down to one
question: how far does it stand above that floor?

182
00:17:41,971 --> 00:17:49,202
That gap is the signal-to-noise ratio — SNR — and it is the
single best predictor of whether the bits come out clean.

183
00:17:49,352 --> 00:17:57,965
The definition is one subtraction: signal power minus
noise-floor power, both in d-B-m, giving a gap in decibels.

184
00:17:57,885 --> 00:18:04,083
A signal at minus eighty-five over a floor at
minus one-oh-five has twenty decibels of SNR.

185
00:18:04,003 --> 00:18:15,124
And because it's a difference, any calibration offset in your receiver cancels out — which is why
SNR means something even on twenty-five dollar hardware that has no idea what a true d-B-m is.

186
00:18:15,274 --> 00:18:17,385
Every mode has a price of admission.

187
00:18:17,305 --> 00:18:22,121
Analog FM voice becomes readable around
ten to twelve decibels of SNR.

188
00:18:22,041 --> 00:18:31,315
The digital voice modes GopherTrunk decodes — C4FM, CQPSK — want
roughly fifteen to twenty at the demodulator for a clean lock.

189
00:18:31,235 --> 00:18:34,264
Below the threshold, things don't degrade
gracefully…

190
00:18:34,414 --> 00:18:35,956
…they fall off a cliff.

191
00:18:35,876 --> 00:18:44,326
Digital links live on a waterfall curve: a few decibels above
threshold, the error rate is negligible — the audio is perfect.

192
00:18:44,246 --> 00:18:47,925
A few decibels below, the link collapses
entirely.

193
00:18:47,845 --> 00:18:53,439
That's why digital radio never gets static-y
like analog; it's flawless, then gone.

194
00:18:53,359 --> 00:19:04,282
Forward error correction — deliberately sending redundant bits — buys the cliff a few
decibels of grace, letting a mode decode below the SNR its raw modulation could survive.

195
00:19:04,432 --> 00:19:10,351
And here's the payoff of the bandwidth chapter's
closing idea: the noise floor isn't fixed.

196
00:19:10,271 --> 00:19:17,026
Noise power scales with the bandwidth you admit — the floor rises
about three decibels every time you double your filter width.

197
00:19:16,946 --> 00:19:26,395
Narrow the filter to hug the signal's occupied bandwidth and you shut
noise out while keeping all the signal: free SNR, no new antenna required.

198
00:19:26,315 --> 00:19:34,614
It also means an SNR figure only really means something at a stated
bandwidth — worth remembering when comparing numbers between systems.

199
00:19:34,764 --> 00:19:44,840
GopherTrunk reports live demodulator SNR and error-vector magnitude for every
channel it decodes, in decibels, precisely so you can see how much margin a link has.

200
00:19:44,760 --> 00:19:52,467
A failed decode with healthy SNR isn't a weak signal —
it's something else: overload, wrong tuning, multipath.

201
00:19:52,387 --> 00:19:54,754
The number tells you which problem you're
hunting.

202
00:19:54,674 --> 00:19:58,921
When you're improving a station, you are
really improving one number — and this is it.

203
00:19:59,871 --> 00:20:12,687
So: SNR is the gap between your signal and the floor, digital modes live or die on a cliff around
fifteen to twenty decibels, and the cheapest SNR you'll ever buy is a filter no wider than the signal.

204
00:20:12,607 --> 00:20:14,532
Full write-up linked below.

205
00:20:18,310 --> 00:20:19,783
And that's the foundation.

206
00:20:19,703 --> 00:20:41,226
Here's the whole course in one breath: a radio wave is a self-propagating ripple with three adjustable properties; frequency is its address and wavelength its size; modulation wiggles
those properties to carry a message; the message takes up bandwidth; decibels let you do the power math in your head; and signal-to-noise ratio decides whether any of it decodes.

207
00:20:41,146 --> 00:20:49,294
Every one of those ideas has a full written article — with the
formulas and sources — in the GopherTrunk Field Guide, linked below.

208
00:20:49,444 --> 00:20:58,498
From here, the next course takes these fundamentals and puts real
hardware on your desk: SDR from Zero — your first software-defined radio.

209
00:20:58,418 --> 00:21:03,002
If this one earned it, subscribing is the
easiest way to catch that release.

